# ===========================================================================
#  THIS FILE IS COMMITTED. Never put a real password in it.
#
#      cp server/.env.example server/.env
#
#  .env is git-ignored and is the only safe place for credentials. Editing
#  this file changes nothing: the app reads .env, not .env.example.
# ===========================================================================
#
# Everything here has a working default except the mail relay, so the app runs
# with this file untouched — it just logs emails instead of sending them.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sending real email
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Without this block, invitations and verification links are written to the
# server log rather than delivered, and the invite toast tells you so.
#
# Gmail, which is what most people have to hand:
#
#   1. Turn on 2-Step Verification:
#      https://myaccount.google.com/signinoptions/twosv
#      App passwords do not exist until this is on. Until then the app
#      passwords page just says "not available for your account", without
#      saying that this is why.
#   2. Create an app password: https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords
#      Pick "Mail" / "Other", name it SyncSpace.
#   3. Paste the 16 characters below exactly as Google shows them. The spaces
#      are display only and are stripped for you.
#
# Then prove it works before trusting it with an invite:
#
#   cd server && npm run mail:check -- you@example.com
#
SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=your.address@gmail.com
SMTP_PASS=abcd efgh ijkl mnop

# The From header. Optional when SMTP_USER is itself an address, which is the
# Gmail case — a relay only accepts a From it recognises anyway.
# MAIL_FROM=SyncSpace <your.address@gmail.com>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# If app passwords stay unavailable
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A school or work Google account whose administrator has switched them off,
# or one on Advanced Protection, will never offer them however the account is
# configured. Any SMTP provider works instead — nothing above is Gmail-specific.
#
# Brevo is the least friction: a free account gives 300 emails a day, needs no
# card, and hands you SMTP credentials directly. Verify your sender address,
# then take the details from https://app.brevo.com/settings/keys/smtp
#
#   SMTP_HOST=smtp-relay.brevo.com
#   SMTP_PORT=587
#   SMTP_USER=<the login it shows you>
#   SMTP_PASS=<the SMTP key it generates>
#
# Set MAIL_FROM explicitly for Brevo. Its login is often something like
# 9a1b2c@smtp-brevo.com, which is an address but not one it will send as — the
# From has to be a sender you verified.
#
#   MAIL_FROM=SyncSpace <your.address@gmail.com>

# A provider that gives you one URL instead of four parts. Use this OR the
# SMTP_HOST block above, never both. Note that a password containing @ or :
# has to be percent-encoded here, which is the reason the parts exist.
# SMTP_URL=smtps://user:pass@mail.example.com:465

# Where emailed links point — the confirmation link, the room invitation and
# the password reset. Defaults to the first CORS_ORIGIN, which is the same app
# in every deployment we run. Set it if the client lives elsewhere.
# CLIENT_URL=http://localhost:5173

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything else
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PORT=4000
# MONGODB_URI=mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/syncspace

# 32+ characters. A development default is used when unset; production refuses
# to start without a real one.
# JWT_SECRET=

# Comma-separated absolute origins, no wildcards. Required in production.
# CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# Running a room's buffer executes a real program on this machine, unsandboxed.
# Worth switching off anywhere the people in a room are not people you trust.
# ALLOW_CODE_EXECUTION=true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generating code from the whiteboard
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The only feature that reaches outside this machine, and the only one that
# costs money per use. Without a key it reports itself unavailable and says
# why — the button is never offered where it cannot work — and reading the
# architecture off the board still works, because that needs no model.
#
# Set ONE of these. Which service gets called is worked out from the shape of
# the key (sk-ant- is Anthropic, AIza is Google), so there is no second
# setting to keep in step with it.
#
#   ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...     https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys
#   GOOGLE_API_KEY=AIza...           https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
#
# AI_ENABLED=true

# Defaults to the provider's own: claude-sonnet-5, or gemini-3.6-flash.
# Note that Google's older 2.5 names are still listed by its models endpoint
# but are closed to new keys, and answer a 404 that reads like the model does
# not exist.
# AI_MODEL=

# Only for a gateway or proxy whose keys look like neither vendor's.
# AI_PROVIDER=anthropic
# AI_BASE_URL=

# A whole change set arrives in one answer. Below about 4000 the last file is
# routinely cut off, which costs the request and produces nothing usable.
# AI_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=8000

# Generation holds an HTTP request open, so this is how long somebody watches
# a spinner before being told it failed.
# AI_TIMEOUT_MS=120000

# Per IP within RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS. Far below the general budget: the cost
# of abuse here is a bill rather than load.
# AI_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=10

# Credential endpoints, per IP within AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS (15 minutes).
# Each has its own budget so a flood against one cannot starve another — a
# login brute force must not lock legitimate people out of signing up, and
# requests for other people's reset links must not stop someone finishing
# their own. Defaults shown; raise them only if a shared NAT makes the real
# budget per office rather than per person.
# AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_LOGIN_MAX=10
# AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_REGISTER_MAX=5
# AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_PASSWORD_CHANGE_MAX=10
# AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_VERIFY_MAX=10
# AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_RESEND_MAX=5
# Asking for a reset link emails somebody else's address, so it is budgeted
# like resending; spending one is a guess at a 256-bit token, so it is
# budgeted like confirming.
# AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_FORGOT_MAX=5
# AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_RESET_MAX=10
# Signing devices out. Higher than the rest: it is authenticated and can only
# reach the caller's own sessions, so the cap is against repeating the
# connection-closing work rather than against guessing.
# AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_SESSION_REVOKE_MAX=30
